helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03040000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153770				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>zooming in on formula display\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas; increasing size of display\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03040000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153770				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03040000.xhp\" name=\"Zoom In\"\>Zoom In\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03040000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490				0	ta	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Increases the display scale of the formula by 25%.\</ahelp\> The current zoom factor is displayed on the status bar. A selection of available zoom options is accessible through the \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000001.xhp#kontextmenue\" name=\"context menu\"\>context menu\</link\>. The context menu in the work area also contains zoom commands.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Brackets and Grouping				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147341				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>brackets and grouping in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>grouping and brackets in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147341				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091100.xhp\" name=\"Brackets and Grouping\"\>Brackets and Grouping\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150342				0	ta	Note: the quotation marks in the examples are used to emphasize text and do not belong to the content of the formulas and commands.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146962				0	ta	When typing example formulas into the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window, note that spaces are often required for correct structure.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149054				0	ta	Braces "{}" are used to group expressions together to form one new expression. For example, "sqrt {x * y}" is the square root of the entire product x*y, while "sqrt x * y" is the square root of x multiplied by y. Braces do not require an extra space.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151392				0	ta	Set brackets were previously inserted in the Elements window or directly in the Commands window as "left lbrace <?> right rbrace". Now, a left and a right set bracket can also be inserted using "lbrace" and "rbrace", with or without wildcards.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147403				0	ta	There are a total of eight (8) different types of brackets available. The "ceil" and "floor" brackets are often used for rounding up or down the argument to the next integer: "lceil -3.7 rceil = -3" or "lfloor -3.7 rfloor = -4".				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146320				0	ta	Operator brackets, also known as Bra-kets (angle brackets with a vertical line in between), are common in Physics notation: "langle a mline b rangle" or "langle a mline b mline c over d mline e rangle." The height and positioning of the vertical lines always corresponds exactly to the enclosing brackets.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157870				0	ta	All brackets may only be used in pairs. The brackets have some common characteristics:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155761				0	ta	All types of brackets have the same grouping function as described for "{}" brackets.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145590				0	ta	All types of brackets, including those that are visible, permit empty group definition. The enclosed expression may therefore be empty.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154562				0	ta	Brackets do not adjust their size to the enclosed expression. For example, if you want "( a over b )" with a bracket size adjusted to a and b you must insert "left" and "right". Entering "left(a over b right)" produces appropriate sizing. If, however, the brackets themselves are part of the expression whose size is changed, they are included the size change: "size 3(a over b)" and "size 12(a over b)". The sizing of the bracket-to-expression ratio does not change in any way.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153002				0	ta	Since "left" and "right" ensure unique assignment of the brackets, every single bracket can be used as an argument for these two commands, even placing right brackets on the left side, or left brackets on the right. Instead of a bracket you can use the "none" qualifier, which means that there is no bracket shown and that there is no space reserved for a bracket. Using this, you can create the following expressions:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150014				0	ta	left lbrace x right none				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149877				0	ta	left [ x right )				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145241				0	ta	left ] x right [				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156060				0	ta	left rangle x right lfloor				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150935				0	ta	The same rules apply to "left" and "right" as to the other brackets: they also work as group builders and may enclose empty expressions.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149030				0	ta	The combination of mismatched brackets, single brackets and repositioned left and right brackets occurs often in mathematical formulas. The following is a formula that will create an error when typed:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155989				0	ta	[2, 3) - right open interval				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147169				0	ta	Using "left" and "right" makes the above expression valid in $[officename] Math: "left [2, 3 right )". However, the brackets do not have any fixed size because they adjust to the argument. Setting a single bracket is a bit cumbersome. Therefore, there you can display single brackets with a fixed size by placing a "\\" (backslash) in front of normal brackets. These brackets then act like any other symbol and no longer have the special functionality of brackets; that is they do not work as group builders and their orientation corresponds to that of other symbols. See "size *2 \\langle x \\rangle" and "size *2 langle x rangle".				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153720				0	ta	The complete overview is as follows				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149715				0	ta	\\{ or \\lbrace, \\} or \\rbrace				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150756				0	ta	\\(, \\)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145207				0	ta	\\[, \\]				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153153				0	ta	\\langle, \\rangle				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150263				0	ta	\\lceil, \\rceil				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147252				0	ta	\\lfloor, \\rfloor				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154690				0	ta	\\lline, \\rline				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145414				0	ta	\\ldline, \\rdline				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147514				0	ta	In this way, intervals like the one above can be built in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\> without any problems: \\[2", "3\\) or "\\]2", "3\\[ (Attention: These quotation marks are part of the entry.)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153532				0	ta	Please note that the quotation marks must be entered and can be obtained with \<emph\>Shift+2\</emph\> and not with typographical quotation marks. Generally, punctuation marks (like the comma in this case) are set as text. Although it is also possible to type "\\[2,~3\\)" the above option is preferable. In the previous example, "fixed size" always describes a bracket size dependent on the font size used.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153674				0	ta	Nesting groups within each other is relatively problem-free. In the formula hat "{a + b}" the "hat" is displayed simply over the center of "{a + b}". Also, "color red lceil a rceil" and "grave hat langle x * y rangle" work as expected. The result of the latter can be compared to "grave {hat langle x * y rangle}." These attributes do not compete, but rather can be combined.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147526				0	ta	This differs slightly for competing or mutually influencing attributes. This is often the case with font attributes. For example, which color does the b have in "color yellow color red (a + color green b)", or which size does it have in "size *4 (a + size /2 b)"? Given a base size of 12, does it have the size 48, 6 or even 24 (which could be seen as a combination)? The following are basic resolution rules, which will be followed consistently in the future. In general, the rules apply to all group operations. This only has a visible effect on the font attributes, like "bold", "ital", "phantom", "size", "color" and "font":				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152952				0	ta	Group operations in sequence are treated as if every single operation is enclosed by braces. They are nested, and in every level there can be no more than one operation. Here is an example of a formula with many group operations: "size 12 color red font sans size -5 (a + size 8 b)" like "{size 12{color red{font sans{size -5 (a + {size 8 b})}}}}".				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3158441				0	ta	This example formula is then interpreted from left to right. The operations only affect its corresponding group (or expression). Operations further to the right "replace" or "combine themselves with" their predecessors.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150994				0	ta	A group operation does not have any effect on higher-level operations but rather affects only lower-level groups and expressions, including their brackets and super-/subscripts. For example, "a + size *2 (b * size -8 c_1)^2"				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154196				0	ta	"color ..." and "font ..." as well as "size n" (n is a decimal) replace any preceding operations of the same type				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154136				0	ta	for "size +n", "size -n", "size *n", and "size /n" the effects of the operations are combined,				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146934				0	ta	"size *2 size -5 a" would be double the starting size minus 5				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149297				0	ta	"font sans ( a + font serif b)"				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155174				0	ta	"size *2 ( a + size /2 b )"				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154906				0	ta	To change the size of a formula, use "size +" or -,*,/. Do not use "size n". These can easily be used in any context. This enables you to copy to other areas by using Copy and Paste, and the result remains the same. Furthermore, such expressions survive a change of base size in the menu better than when using "size n". If you use only "size *" and "size /" (for example, "size *1.24 a or size /0.86 a") the proportions remain intact.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147587				0	ta	Examples (with a base size of 12 and 50% for indexes):				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148734				0	ta	Exactly identical proportions with "size 18 a_n" and "size *1.5 a_n".				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152766				0	ta	This differs in different contexts: "x^{size 18 a_n}" and "x^{size *1.5 a_n}"				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157986				0	ta	Examples with size +n for a comparison. They look identical:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3158001				0	ta	a_{size 8 n}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147332				0	ta	a_{size +2 n}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155143				0	ta	a_{size *1.333 n}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147129				0	ta	The following examples, however, do not look identical:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147073				0	ta	x^{a_{size 8 n}}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147086				0	ta	x^{a_{size +2 n}}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154386				0	ta	x^{a_{size *1.333 n}}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153354				0	ta	Note that all n here have different sizes. The size 1.333 results from 8/6, the desired size divided by the default index size 6. (Index size 50% with a base size of 12)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03070000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Update				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03070000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153768				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>updating formula view\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formula view; updating\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03070000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153768				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03070000.xhp\" name=\"Update\"\>Update\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03070000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153729				0	ta	\<ahelp hid=\"SID_DRAW\"\>This command updates the formula in the document window.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03070000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145253				0	ta	Changes in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window are automatically updated if \<emph\>AutoUpdate Display\</emph\> is activated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Unary/Binary Operators				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150342				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>unary operators\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>binary operators\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>operators; unary and binary\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>plus signs\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>plus/minus signs\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>minus/plus signs\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>multiplication signs\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>NOT operator\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>AND operator\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>logical operators\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Boolean operators\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>OR operator\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>concatenating math symbols\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>addition signs\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>subtraction signs\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>minus signs\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>slash division sign\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>backslash division sign\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>indexes; adding to formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>powers\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>division signs\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>user-defined operators;unary and binary\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150342				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03090100.xhp\" name=\"Unary/Binary Operators\"\>Unary/Binary Operators\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151241				0	ta	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SMA_UNBINOPS_CAT\"\>You can choose various unary and binary operators to build your $[officename] Math formula. Unary refers to operators that affect one placeholder. Binary refers to operators that connect two placeholders. The lower area of the Elements window displays the individual operators.\</ahelp\> The \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000001.xhp#kontextmenue\" name=\"context menu\"\>context menu\</link\> of the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window also contains a list of these operators, as well as additional operators. If you need an operator that is not contained in the Elements window, use the context menu or type it directly in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146963				0	ta	The following is a complete list of the unary and binary operators. The symbol next to the operator indicates that it can be accessed through the Elements window (choose \<emph\>View - Elements\</emph\>) or through the context menu of the Commands window.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147405				0	ta	Unary and Binary Operators				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090100.xhp	0	help	par_idN10085				0	ta	\<image id=\"img_id3156399\" src=\"starmath/res/un21201.png\" width=\"0.3335inch\" height=\"0.3335inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3156399\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
