helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\02090000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153770				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>markers; previous\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>placeholders; previous marker\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\02090000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153770				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/02090000.xhp\" name=\"Previous Marker\"\>Previous Marker\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\02090000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145252				0	ta	\<ahelp hid=\"SID_PREVMARK\" visibility=\"visible\"\>Moves the cursor to the previous marker (to the left).\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\02090000.xhp	0	help	par_id3148488				0	ta	"Markers" are placeholders. They take the form of <?> in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Indexes and Exponents				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150746				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>indexes and exponents in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>exponents and indexes in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150746				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091200.xhp\" name=\"Indexes and Exponents\"\>Indexes and Exponents\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153730				0	ta	Here, you will find basic information about indexes and exponents in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\>. You can try the examples described here to help you understand the details discussed. (The quotation marks in this text are for emphasis purposes only and are not part of the examples.)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149884				0	ta	The index and exponent for a character are displayed one on top of the other, left-justified to the base character. For example, type \<emph\>a_2^3\</emph\> or \<emph\>a^3_2\</emph\>. This can be in any order. Instead of \<emph\>'_'\</emph\> and \<emph\>'^'\</emph\>, you can use \<emph\>'sub'\</emph\> and \<emph\>'sup'\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148387				0	ta	However, it is no longer possible to use the following patterns				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149029				0	ta	a_2_3				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155985				0	ta	a^2^3				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153923				0	ta	a_2^3_4				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153724				0	ta	Each sub-/superscript position of a base character can only be used once. You must use brackets to indicate the desired result. The following examples illustrate this				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151185				0	ta	a_{2_3}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151272				0	ta	a^{2^3}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156316				0	ta	a_2^{3_4}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145207				0	ta	a_{2^3}^{4_5}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151173	13			0	ta	Unlike other formula editors where "\<emph\>_\</emph\>" and " \<emph\>^\</emph\> " only refer to the next character ("a_24" refers only to the "2"), $[officename] Math refers to the entire number(s)/name(s)/text. If you want to put superscripts and subscripts in sequence, the expression can be written as follows: a_2{}^3 or a^3{}_2				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154260				0	ta	To write tensors, \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\> provides several options. In addition to the notation "R_i{}^{jk}{}_l", common in other applications, additional notations can be used, namely "R_i{}^jk{}_l" and "{{R_i}^jk}_l."				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147516				0	ta	Super- and subscripts to the left of the base character can also be right-justified. To do this, the new commands "lsub" and "lsup" are used. Both commands have the same effect as "sub" and "sup", except that they are left of the base character. See also "a lsub 2 lsup 3."				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154276				0	ta	The rules governing unambiguity and the necessity of using brackets remain the same. In principle, this can be achieved with \<emph\>{}_2^3 a\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152961				0	ta	The commands "sub" and "sup" are also available as "rsub" and "rsup".				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3158437				0	ta	Using the "csub" and "csup" commands, you can write super- and subscripts directly above or below a character. An example is "a csub y csup x". Combinations of indexes and exponents together are also possible: "abc_1^2 lsub 3 lsup 4 csub 55555 csup 66666."				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154570				0	ta	Super- and subscripts can be attached to most unary and binary operators. Two examples: "a div_2 b a<csub n b +_2 h" and "a toward csub f b x toward csup f y."				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155904				0	ta	Be sure to also enter all spaces between characters when entering these examples into the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.				20200411 15:09:53
