helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Defining Database Ranges				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154758				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>tables; database ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>database ranges; defining\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>ranges; defining database ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>defining;database ranges\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154758				0	ta	\<variable id=\"database_define\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/database_define.xhp\" name=\"Defining Database Ranges\"\>Defining a Database Range\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3153768				0	ta	You can define a range of cells in a spreadsheet to use as a database. Each row in this database range corresponds to a database record and each cell in a row corresponds to a database field. You can sort, group, search, and perform calculations on the range as you would in a database.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3145801	82			0	ta	You can only edit and access a database range in the spreadsheet that contains the range. You cannot access the database range in the %PRODUCTNAME Data Sources view. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_idN10648				0	ta	To define a database range				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3155064				0	ta	Select the range of cells that you want to define as a database range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_idN10654				0	ta	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Define Range\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3153715				0	ta	In the \<emph\>Name\</emph\> box, enter a name for the database range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066A				0	ta	Click \<emph\>More\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253				0	ta	Specify the options for the database range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\database_define.xhp	0	help	par_idN10675				0	ta	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_cellmerge.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Merging and Splitting Cells				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_cellmerge.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147240				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>cells; merging/unmerging\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>tables; merging cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell merges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>unmerging cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>merging;cells\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_cellmerge.xhp	0	help	hd_id8005005				0	ta	\<variable id=\"table_cellmerge\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/table_cellmerge.xhp\" name=\"Merging and Unmerging Cells\"\>Merging and Unmerging Cells\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_cellmerge.xhp	0	help	par_id8049867				0	ta	You can select adjacent cells, then merge them into a single cell. Conversely, you can take a large cell that has been created by merging single cells, and divide it back into individual cells.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_cellmerge.xhp	0	help	par_id0509200913480176				0	ta	When you copy cells into a target range containing merged cells, the target range gets unmerged first, then the copied cells are pasted in. If the copied cells are merged cells, they retain their merge state.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_cellmerge.xhp	0	help	hd_id235602				0	ta	Merging Cells				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_cellmerge.xhp	0	help	par_id1272927				0	ta	Select the adjacent cells.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_cellmerge.xhp	0	help	par_id6424146				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>Format - Merge Cells\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_cellmerge.xhp	0	help	hd_id451368				0	ta	Canceling the merging of cells				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_cellmerge.xhp	0	help	par_id7116611				0	ta	Place the cursor in the cell to be split.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_cellmerge.xhp	0	help	par_id9493087				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>Format - Merge Cells\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Selecting Multiple Cells				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153361				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>cells; selecting\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>marking cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>selecting;cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>multiple cells selection\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>selection modes in spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>tables; selecting ranges\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153361				0	ta	\<variable id=\"mark_cells\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/mark_cells.xhp\" name=\"Selecting Multiple Cells\"\>Selecting Multiple Cells\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145272				0	ta	Select a rectangular range				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3149261				0	ta	With the mouse button pressed, drag from one corner to the diagonally opposed corner of the range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151119				0	ta	Mark a single cell				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3146975				0	ta	Do one of the following:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3163710				0	ta	Click, then Shift-click the cell.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3149959				0	ta	Pressing the mouse button, drag a range across two cells, do not release the mouse button, and then drag back to the first cell. Release the mouse button. You can now move the individual cell by drag and drop.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154942				0	ta	Select various dispersed cells				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id1001200901072060				0	ta	Do one of the following:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3156284				0	ta	Mark at least one cell. Then while pressing \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>, click each of the additional cells.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id1001200901072023				0	ta	Click the STD / EXT / ADD area in the status bar until it shows ADD. Now click all cells that you want to select.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146971				0	ta	Switch marking mode				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3155064				0	ta	On the status bar, click the box with the legend STD / EXT / ADD to switch the marking mode:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3159264				0	ta	Field contents				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3155337				0	ta	Effect of clicking the mouse				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3149568				0	ta	STD				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3148486				0	ta	A mouse click selects the cell you have clicked on. Unmarks all marked cells.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3150090				0	ta	EXT				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3150305				0	ta	A mouse click marks a rectangular range from the current cell to the cell you clicked. Alternatively, Shift-click a cell.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3145587				0	ta	ADD				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3154368				0	ta	A mouse click in a cell adds it to the already marked cells. A mouse click in a marked cell unmarks it. Alternatively, \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>-click the cells.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\mark_cells.xhp	0	help	par_id3154487				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/scalc/main0208.xhp\" name=\"Status bar\"\>Status bar\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Applying Sort Lists				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150870				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>filling;customized lists\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sort lists;applying\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defining;sort lists\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>geometric lists\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>arithmetic lists\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>series;sort lists\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>lists; user-defined\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>customized lists\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150870	3			0	ta	\<variable id=\"sorted_list\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/sorted_list.xhp\" name=\"Applying Sort Lists\"\>Applying Sort Lists\</link\> \</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	par_id3159154				0	ta	Sort lists allow you to type one piece of information in a cell, then drag it to fill in a consecutive list of items.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	par_id3148645				0	ta	For example, enter the text "Jan" or "January" in an empty cell. Select the cell and click the mouse on the lower right corner of the cell border. Then drag the selected cell a few cells to the right or downwards. When you release the mouse button, the highlighted cells will be filled with the names of the months.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	par_id2367931				0	ta	Hold down \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>if you do not want to fill the cells with different values.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	par_id3152577				0	ta	The predefined series can be found under \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - Sort Lists\</emph\>. You can also create your own lists of text strings tailored to your needs, such as a list of your company's branch offices. When you use the information in these lists later (for example, as headings), just enter the first name in the list and expand the entry by dragging it with your mouse.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\sorted_list.xhp	0	help	par_id3147434				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060400.xhp\" name=\"Sort lists\"\>Sort lists\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Converting Text to Numbers				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145068				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>formats; text as numbers\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>time format conversion\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>date formats;conversion\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>converting;text, into numbers\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	hd_id0908200901265171				0	ta	\<variable id=\"numbers_text\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/numbers_text.xhp\" name=\"Converting Text to Numbers\"\>Converting Text to Numbers\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265127				0	ta	Calc converts text inside cells to the respective numeric values if an unambiguous conversion is possible. If no conversion is possible, Calc returns a #VALUE! error.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265196				0	ta	Only integer numbers including exponent are converted, and ISO 8601 dates and times in their extended formats with separators. Anything else, like fractional numbers with decimal separators or dates other than ISO 8601, is not converted, as the text string would be locale dependent. Leading and trailing blanks are ignored.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265220				0	ta	The following ISO 8601 formats are converted:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265288				0	ta	CCYY-MM-DD				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265267				0	ta	CCYY-MM-DDThh:mm				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265248				0	ta	CCYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265374				0	ta	CCYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss,s				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265327				0	ta	CCYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss.s				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265399				0	ta	hh:mm				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265347				0	ta	hh:mm:ss				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265349				0	ta	hh:mm:ss,s				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265342				0	ta	hh:mm:ss.s				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265491				0	ta	The century code CC may not be omitted. Instead of the T date and time separator, exactly one space character may be used.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265467				0	ta	If a date is given, it must be a valid Gregorian calendar date. In this case the optional time must be in the range 00:00 to 23:59:59.99999...				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265420				0	ta	If only a time string is given, it may have an hours value of more than 24, while minutes and seconds can have a maximum value of 59.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265448				0	ta	The conversion is done for single arguments only, as in =A1+A2, or ="1E2"+1. Cell range arguments are not affected, so SUM(A1:A2) differs from A1+A2 if at least one of the two cells contain a convertible string.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id090820090126540				0	ta	Strings inside formulas are also converted, such as in ="1999-11-22"+42, which returns the date 42 days after November 22nd, 1999. Calculations involving localized dates as strings inside the formula return an error. For example, the localized date string "11/22/1999" or "22.11.1999" cannot be used for the automatic conversion.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	hd_id1005200903485368				0	ta	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id1005200903485359				0	ta	In A1 enter the text \<item type=\"literal\"\>'1e2\</item\> (which is converted to the number 100 internally).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id1005200903485341				0	ta	In A2 enter \<item type=\"literal\"\>=A1+1\</item\> (which correctly results in 101).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\numbers_text.xhp	0	help	par_id0908200901265544				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/05020300.xhp\" name=\"Format - Cells - Numbers\"\>Format - Cells - Numbers\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_minusvalue.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Highlighting Negative Numbers				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_minusvalue.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147434				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>negative numbers\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>numbers; highlighting negative numbers\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>highlighting;negative numbers\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>colors;negative numbers\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>number formats;colors for negative numbers\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_minusvalue.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147434				0	ta	\<variable id=\"cellstyle_minusvalue\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cellstyle_minusvalue.xhp\" name=\"Highlighting Negative Numbers\"\>Highlighting Negative Numbers\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_minusvalue.xhp	0	help	par_id3153878				0	ta	You can format cells with a number format that highlights negative numbers in red. Alternatively, you can define your own number format in which negative numbers are highlighted in other colors.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_minusvalue.xhp	0	help	par_id3155600				0	ta	Select the cells and choose \<emph\>Format - Cells\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_minusvalue.xhp	0	help	par_id3146969				0	ta	On the \<emph\>Numbers\</emph\> tab, select a number format and mark \<emph\>Negative numbers red\</emph\> check box. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_minusvalue.xhp	0	help	par_id3145640				0	ta	The cell number format is defined in two parts. The format for positive numbers and zero is defined in front of the semicolon; after the semicolon the formula for negative numbers is defined. You can change the code (RED) under \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format code\</item\>. For example, instead of RED, enter \<item type=\"literal\"\>YELLOW\</item\>. If the new code appears in the list after clicking the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Add\</item\> icon, this is a valid entry.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Referencing a Cell in Another Document				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147436				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>sheet references\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>references; to cells in other sheets/documents\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cells; operating in another document\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>documents;references\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147436				0	ta	\<variable id=\"cellreferences\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cellreferences.xhp\" name=\"Referencing Other Sheets\"\>Referencing Other Sheets\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id9663075				0	ta	In a sheet cell you can show a reference to a cell in another sheet.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id1879329				0	ta	In the same way, a reference can also be made to a cell from another document provided that this document has already been saved as a file.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	hd_id7122409				0	ta	To Reference a Cell in the Same Document				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id2078005				0	ta	Open a new, empty spreadsheet.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id4943693				0	ta	By way of example, enter the following formula in cell A1 of Sheet1:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id9064302				0	ta	               \<item type=\"literal\"\>=Sheet2.A1\</item\>            				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id7609790				0	ta	Click the \<emph\>Sheet 2\</emph\> tab at the bottom of the spreadsheet. Set the cursor in cell A1 there and enter text or a number.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id809961				0	ta	If you switch back to Sheet1, you will see the same content in cell A1 there. If the contents of Sheet2.A1 change, then the contents of Sheet1.A1 also change.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	hd_id9209570				0	ta	To Reference a Cell in Another Document				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id5949278				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>File - Open\</emph\>, to load an existing spreadsheet document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id8001953				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>File - New\</emph\>, to open a new spreadsheet document. Set the cursor in the cell where you want to insert the external data and enter an equals sign to indicate that you want to begin a formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id8571123				0	ta	Now switch to the document you have just loaded. Click the cell with the data that you want to insert in the new document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id8261665				0	ta	Switch back to the new spreadsheet. In the input line you will now see how $[officename] Calc has added the reference to the formula for you. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id5888241				0	ta	The reference to a cell of another document contains the name of the other document in single inverted commas, then a hash #, then the name of the sheet of the other document, followed by a point and the name of the cell.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id7697683				0	ta	Confirm the formula by clicking the green check mark.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id7099826				0	ta	If you drag the box in the lower right corner of the active cell to select a range of cells, $[officename] automatically inserts the corresponding references in the adjacent cells. As a result, the sheet name is preceded with a "$" sign to designate it as an absolute reference.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreferences.xhp	0	help	par_id674459				0	ta	If you examine the name of the other document in this formula, you will notice that it is written as a \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000002.xhp#url\" name=\"URL\"\>URL\</link\>. This means that you can also enter a URL from the Internet.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Applying Multiple Operations				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147559				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>multiple operations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>what if operations;two variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>tables; multiple operations in\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data tables; multiple operations in\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cross-classified tables\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147559				0	ta	\<variable id=\"multioperation\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/multioperation.xhp\" name=\"Applying Multiple Operations\"\>Applying Multiple Operations\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145171				0	ta	Multiple Operations in Columns or Rows				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id4123966				0	ta	The \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Multiple Operations\</item\> command provides a planning tool for "what if" questions. In your spreadsheet, you enter a formula to calculate a result from values that are stored in other cells. Then, you set up a cell range where you enter some fixed values, and the Multiple Operations command will calculate the results depending on the formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3156424				0	ta	In the \<emph\>Formulas\</emph\> field, enter the cell reference to the formula that applies to the data range. In the \<emph\>Column input cell/Row input cell\</emph\> field, enter the cell reference to the corresponding cell that is part of the formula. This can be explained best by examples:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159153				0	ta	Examples				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3153189				0	ta	You produce toys which you sell for $10 each. Each toy costs $2 to make, in addition to which you have fixed costs of $10,000 per year. How much profit will you make in a year if you sell a particular number of toys?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id6478774				0	ta	\<image id=\"img_id1621753\" src=\"res/helpimg/what-if.png\" width=\"0.2228in\" height=\"0.2228in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id1621753\"\>what-if sheet area\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145239				0	ta	Calculating With One Formula and One Variable				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3146888				0	ta	To calculate the profit, first enter any number as the quantity (items sold) - in this example 2000. The profit is found from the formula Profit=Quantity * (Selling price - Direct costs) - Fixed costs. Enter this formula in B5.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3157875				0	ta	In column D enter given annual sales, one below the other; for example, 500 to 5000, in steps of 500.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3159115				0	ta	Select the range D2:E11, and thus the values in column D and the empty cells alongside in column E.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3149723				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>Data - Multiple operations\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3149149	46			0	ta	With the cursor in the \<emph\>Formulas \</emph\>field, click cell B5.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3149355				0	ta	Set the cursor in the \<emph\>Column input cell\</emph\> field and click cell B4. This means that B4, the quantity, is the variable in the formula, which is replaced by the selected column values.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3149009				0	ta	Close the dialog with \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. You see the profits for the different quantities in column E.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148725				0	ta	Calculating with Several Formulas Simultaneously				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3146880				0	ta	Delete column E.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3154675				0	ta	Enter the following formula in C5: = B5 / B4. You are now calculating the annual profit per item sold.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3148885				0	ta	Select the range D2:F11, thus three columns.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3147474				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>Data - Multiple Operations\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3154846				0	ta	With the cursor in the \<emph\>Formulas\</emph\> field, select cells B5 through C5.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3153931				0	ta	Set the cursor in the \<emph\>Column input cell\</emph\> field and click cell B4.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3150862				0	ta	Close the dialog with \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. You will now see the profits in column E and the annual profit per item in column F.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146139				0	ta	Multiple Operations Across Rows and Columns				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3148584				0	ta	\<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> allows you to carry out joint multiple operations for columns and rows in so-called cross-tables. The formula cell has to refer to both the data range arranged in rows and the one arranged in columns. Select the range defined by both data ranges and call the multiple operation dialog. Enter the reference to the formula in the \<emph\>Formulas\</emph\> field. The \<emph\>Row input cell\</emph\> and the \<emph\>Column input cell\</emph\> fields are used to enter the reference to the corresponding cells of the formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149949				0	ta	Calculating with Two Variables				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3154808				0	ta	Consider columns A and B of the sample table above. You now want to vary not just the quantity produced annually, but also the selling price, and you are interested in the profit in each case.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3149731				0	ta	Expand the table shown above. D2 through D11 contain the numbers 500, 1000 and so on, up to 5000. In E1 through H1 enter the numbers 8, 10, 15 and 20.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3152810				0	ta	Select the range D1:H11.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3153620				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>Data - Multiple Operations\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3149981				0	ta	With the cursor in the \<emph\>Formulas\</emph\> field, click cell B5.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3156113				0	ta	Set the cursor in the \<emph\>Row input cell\</emph\> field and click cell B1. This means that B1, the selling price, is the horizontally entered variable (with the values 8, 10, 15 and 20).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3154049				0	ta	Set the cursor in the \<emph\>Column input cell\</emph\> field and click in B4. This means that B4, the quantity, is the vertically entered variable.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3149141				0	ta	Close the dialog with OK. You see the profits for the different selling prices in the range E2:H11.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multioperation.xhp	0	help	par_id3155104				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/12060000.xhp\" name=\"Multiple operations\"\>Multiple operations\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	 Importing and Exporting CSV Files 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	bm_id892361				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>number series import\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data series import\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>exporting; tables as text\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>importing; tables as text\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>delimited values and files\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>comma separated files and values\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>text file import and export\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>csv files;importing and exporting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>tables; importing/exporting as text\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>text documents; importing to spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>opening;text csv files\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>saving;as text csv\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10862				0	ta	\<variable id=\"csv_files\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/csv_files.xhp\"\>Opening and Saving Text CSV Files\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10880				0	ta	Comma Separated Values (CSV) is a text file format that you can use to exchange data from a database or a spreadsheet between applications. Each line in a Text CSV file represents a record in the database, or a row in a spreadsheet. Each field in a database record or cell in a spreadsheet row is usually separated by a comma. However, you can use other characters to delimit a field, such as a tabulator character.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10886				0	ta	If the field or cell contains a comma, the field or cell \<emph\>must\</emph\> be enclosed by single quotes (') or double quotes (").				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10890				0	ta	To Open a Text CSV File in Calc				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10897				0	ta	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>File - Open\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN1089F				0	ta	Locate the CSV file that you want to open.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN108A2				0	ta	If the file has a *.csv extension, select the file.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN108A5				0	ta	If the CSV file has another extension, select the file, and then select "Text CSV" in the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>File type\</item\> box				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN1082D				0	ta	Click \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Open\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10834				0	ta	The \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Text Import\</item\> dialog opens.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN108B1				0	ta	Specify the options to divide the text in the file into columns.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN108BB				0	ta	You can preview the layout of the imported data at the bottom of the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Text Import\</item\> dialog. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_id8444166				0	ta	Right-click a column in the preview to set the format or to hide the column.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN108E2				0	ta	Check the text delimiter box that matches the character used as text delimiter in the file. In case of an unlisted delimiter, type the character into the input box.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN108C5				0	ta	Click \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>OK\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN108FA				0	ta	To Save a Sheet as a Text CSV File				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN106FC				0	ta	When you export a spreadsheet to CSV format, only the data on the current sheet is saved. All other information, including formulas and formatting, is lost.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10901				0	ta	Open the Calc sheet that you want to save as a Text CSV file.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN107AF				0	ta	Only the current sheet can be exported.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10905				0	ta	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>File - Save as\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN10915				0	ta	In the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>File name\</item\> box, enter a name for the file.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN1090D				0	ta	In the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>File type\</item\> box, select "Text CSV".				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN107DD				0	ta	(Optional) Set the field options for the Text CSV file.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN1091C				0	ta	Select \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Edit filter settings\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN107ED				0	ta	In the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Export of text files\</item\> dialog, select the options that you want.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN107F4				0	ta	Click \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>OK\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_idN107FC				0	ta	Click \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Save\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_id3153487				0	ta	\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060100.xhp\" name=\"Spreadsheet - View\"\>%PRODUCTNAME Calc - View\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_id3153008				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000207.xhp\" name=\"Export text files\"\>Export text files\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\csv_files.xhp	0	help	par_id3155595				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000208.xhp\" name=\"Import text files\"\>Import text files\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Rotating Text				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151112				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>cells; rotating text\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>rotating; text in cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>text in cells; writing vertically\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151112				0	ta	\<variable id=\"text_rotate\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/text_rotate.xhp\" name=\"Rotating Text\"\>Rotating Text\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3145171				0	ta	Select the cells whose text you want to rotate.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155133				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>Format - Cells\</emph\>. You will see the \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155854				0	ta	Click the \<emph\>Alignment\</emph\> tab.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3147426				0	ta	In the \<emph\>Text orientation\</emph\> area use the mouse to select in the preview wheel the direction in which the text is to be rotated. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3148456				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05020000.xhp\" name=\"Format - Cells\"\>Format - Cells\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3154944				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/05340300.xhp\" name=\"Format - Cells - Alignment\"\>Format - Cells - Alignment\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Using Print Ranges on a Spreadsheet				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	bm_id14648				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>exporting;cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>printing; cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>ranges;print ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PDF export of print ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell ranges; printing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; print ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>print ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>clearing, see also deleting/removing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defining;print ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>extending print ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>deleting;print ranges\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN108D7				0	ta	\<variable id=\"printranges\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/printranges.xhp\"\>Defining Print Ranges on a Sheet\</link\> \</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN108F5				0	ta	You can define which range of cells on a spreadsheet to print.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN108FB				0	ta	The cells on the sheet that are not part of the defined print range are not printed or exported. Sheets without a defined print range are not printed and not exported to a PDF file, unless the document uses the Excel file format.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN1077A				0	ta	For files opened in Excel format, all sheets that do not contain a defined print range are printed. The same behavior occurs when you export the Excel formatted spreadsheet to a PDF file.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN108FE				0	ta	To Define a Print Range				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10905				0	ta	Select the cells that you want to print.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10909				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>Format - Print Ranges - Define\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10910				0	ta	To Add Cells to a Print Range				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10917				0	ta	Select the cells that you want to add to the existing print range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN1091B				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>Format - Print Ranges - Add\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10922				0	ta	To Clear a Print Range				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10929				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>Format - Print Ranges - Remove\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10953				0	ta	Using the Page Break Preview to Edit Print Ranges				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN1093E				0	ta	In the \<emph\>Page Break Preview\</emph\>, print ranges as well as page break regions are outlined by a blue border and contain a centered page number in gray. Nonprinting areas have a gray background.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_id3153143				0	ta	To define a new page break region, drag the border to a new location. When you define a new page break region, an automatic page break is replaced by a manual page break.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10930				0	ta	To View and Edit Print Ranges				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10937				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>View - Page Break Preview\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN1082A				0	ta	To change the default zoom factor of the \<emph\>Page Break Preview\</emph\>, double click the percentage value on the \<emph\>Status\</emph\> bar, and select a new zoom factor.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10836				0	ta	Edit the print range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10944				0	ta	To change the size of a print range, drag a border of the range to a new location.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_id3151075				0	ta	To delete a manual page break that is contained in a print range, drag the border of the page break outside of the print range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10948				0	ta	To clear a print range, drag a border of the range onto the opposite border of the range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN10862				0	ta	To exit the \<emph\>Page Break Preview\</emph\>, choose \<emph\>View - Normal\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\printranges.xhp	0	help	par_idN109CF				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05080300.xhp\"\>Editing Print Ranges\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Defining Number of Pages for Printing				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153194				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>printing; sheet counts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sheets; printing sheet counts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>page breaks; spreadsheet preview\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>editing;print ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>viewing;print ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>previews;page breaks for printing\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153194				0	ta	\<variable id=\"print_exact\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/print_exact.xhp\" name=\"Defining Number of Pages for Printing\"\>Defining Number of Pages for Printing\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	par_id3153771				0	ta	If a sheet is too large for a single printed page, $[officename] Calc will print the current sheet evenly divided over several pages. Since the automatic page break does not always take place in the optimal position, you can define the page distribution yourself.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	par_id3159155				0	ta	Go to the sheet to be printed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	par_id3150012				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>View - Page Break Preview\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	par_id3146974				0	ta	You will see the automatic distribution of the sheet across the print pages. The automatically created print ranges are indicated by dark blue lines, and the user-defined ones by light blue lines. The page breaks (line breaks and column breaks) are marked as black lines.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	par_id3152578				0	ta	You can move the blue lines with the mouse. You will find further options in the Context menu, including adding an additional print range, removing the scaling and inserting additional manual line and column breaks.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	par_id3151073				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/03100000.xhp\" name=\"View - Page Break Preview\"\>View - Page Break Preview\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Addresses and References, Absolute and Relative				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156423				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>addressing; relative and absolute\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>references; absolute/relative\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>absolute addresses in spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>relative addresses\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>absolute references in spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>relative references\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>references; to cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; references\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156423				0	ta	\<variable id=\"relativ_absolut_ref\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/relativ_absolut_ref.xhp\" name=\"Addresses and References, Absolute and Relative\"\>Addresses and References, Absolute and Relative\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	hd_id3163712				0	ta	Relative Addressing				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3146119				0	ta	The cell in column A, row 1 is addressed as A1. You can address a range of adjacent cells by first entering the coordinates of the upper left cell of the area, then a colon followed by the coordinates of the lower right cell. For example, the square formed by the first four cells in the upper left corner is addressed as A1:B2.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3154730				0	ta	By addressing an area in this way, you are making a relative reference to A1:B2. Relative here means that the reference to this area will be adjusted automatically when you copy the formulas.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149377				0	ta	Absolute Addressing				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3154943				0	ta	Absolute references are the opposite of relative addressing. A dollar sign is placed before each letter and number in an absolute reference, for example, $A$1:$B$2.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3147338				0	ta	$[officename] can convert the current reference, in which the cursor is positioned in the input line, from relative to absolute and vice versa by pressing Shift +F4. If you start with a relative address such as A1, the first time you press this key combination, both row and column are set to absolute references ($A$1). The second time, only the row (A$1), and the third time, only the column ($A1). If you press the key combination once more, both column and row references are switched back to relative (A1)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3153963				0	ta	$[officename] Calc shows the references to a formula. If, for example you click the formula =SUM(A1:C5;D15:D24) in a cell, the two referenced areas in the sheet will be highlighted in color. For example, the formula component "A1:C5" may be in blue and the cell range in question bordered in the same shade of blue. The next formula component "D15:D24" can be marked in red in the same way.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154704				0	ta	When to Use Relative and Absolute References				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3147346				0	ta	What distinguishes a relative reference? Assume you want to calculate in cell E1 the sum of the cells in range A1:B2. The formula to enter into E1 would be: =SUM(A1:B2). If you later decide to insert a new column in front of column A, the elements you want to add would then be in B1:C2 and the formula would be in F1, not in E1. After inserting the new column, you would therefore have to check and correct all formulas in the sheet, and possibly in other sheets.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3155335				0	ta	Fortunately, $[officename] does this work for you. After having inserted a new column A, the formula =SUM(A1:B2) will be automatically updated to =SUM(B1:C2). Row numbers will also be automatically adjusted when a new row 1 is inserted. Absolute and relative references are always adjusted in $[officename] Calc whenever the referenced area is moved. But be careful if you are copying a formula since in that case only the relative references will be adjusted, not the absolute references.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3145791				0	ta	Absolute references are used when a calculation refers to one specific cell in your sheet. If a formula that refers to exactly this cell is copied relatively to a cell below the original cell, the reference will also be moved down if you did not define the cell coordinates as absolute.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3147005				0	ta	Aside from when new rows and columns are inserted, references can also change when an existing formula referring to particular cells is copied to another area of the sheet. Assume you entered the formula =SUM(A1:A9) in row 10. If you want to calculate the sum for the adjacent column to the right, simply copy this formula to the cell to the right. The copy of the formula in column B will be automatically adjusted to =SUM(B1:B9).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Editing Pivot Tables				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148663				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>pivot table function; editing tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>editing;pivot tables\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148663				0	ta	\<variable id=\"datapilot_edittable\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/datapilot_edittable.xhp\" name=\"Editing Pivot Tables\"\>Editing Pivot Tables\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3150868				0	ta	Click one of the buttons in the pivot table and hold the mouse button down. A special symbol will appear next to the mouse pointer.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3145786				0	ta	By dragging the button to a different position in the same row you can alter the order of the columns. If you drag a button to the left edge of the table into the row headings area, you can change a column into a row.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id1648915				0	ta	In the Pivot Table dialog, you can drag a button to the \<emph\>Page Fields\</emph\> area to create a button and a listbox on top of the pivot table. The listbox can be used to filter the pivot table by the contents of the selected item. You can use drag-and-drop within the pivot table to use another page field as a filter.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3147434				0	ta	To remove a button from the table, just drag it out of the pivot table. Release the mouse button when the mouse pointer positioned within the sheet has become a 'not allowed' icon. The button is deleted.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3156442				0	ta	To edit the pivot table, click a cell inside the pivot table and open the context menu. In the context menu you find the command \<emph\>Edit Layout\</emph\>, which displays the \<emph\>Pivot Table\</emph\> dialog for the current pivot table.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id2666096				0	ta	In the pivot table, you can use drag-and-drop or cut/paste commands to rearrange the order of data fields.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id266609688				0	ta	You can assign custom display names to fields, field members, subtotals (with some restrictions), and grand totals inside pivot tables. A custom display name is assigned to an item by overwriting the original name with another name.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Consolidating Data				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150791				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>consolidating data\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>ranges; combining\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>combining;cell ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>tables; combining\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>data; merging cell ranges\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>merging;data ranges\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150791				0	ta	\<variable id=\"consolidate\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/consolidate.xhp\" name=\"Consolidating Data\"\>Consolidating Data\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153191				0	ta	During consolidation, the contents of the cells from several sheets will be combined in one place.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	hd_id892056				0	ta	To Combine Cell Contents				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3151073				0	ta	Open the document that contains the cell ranges to be consolidated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3154513				0	ta	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Consolidate\</item\> to open the \<emph\>Consolidate\</emph\> dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3147345				0	ta	From the \<emph\>Source data area\</emph\> box select a source cell range to consolidate with other areas.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3149209				0	ta	If the range is not named, click in the field next to the \<emph\>Source data area\</emph\>. A blinking text cursor appears. Type a reference for the first source data range or select the range with the mouse.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155529				0	ta	Click \<emph\>Add\</emph\> to insert the selected range in the \<emph\>Consolidation areas\</emph\> field.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153816				0	ta	Select additional ranges and click \<emph\>Add\</emph\> after each selection.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3157983				0	ta	Specify where you want to display the result by selecting a target range from the \<emph\>Copy results to\</emph\> box.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3150215				0	ta	If the target range is not named, click in the field next to \<emph\>Copy results to\</emph\> and enter the reference of the target range. Alternatively, you can select the range using the mouse or position the cursor in the top left cell of the target range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153813				0	ta	Select a function from the \<emph\>Function\</emph\> box. The function specifies how the values of the consolidation ranges are linked. The "Sum" function is the default setting.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3149315				0	ta	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\> to consolidate the ranges.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_idN107DE				0	ta	Additional Settings				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3147250				0	ta	Click \<emph\>More\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Consolidate\</emph\> dialog to display additional settings:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3156400				0	ta	Select \<emph\>Link to source data\</emph\> to insert the formulas that generate the results in the target range, rather than the actual results. If you link the data, any values modified in the source range are automatically updated in the target range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3150538				0	ta	The corresponding cell references in the target range are inserted in consecutive rows, which are automatically ordered and then hidden from view. Only the final result, based on the selected function, is displayed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3149945				0	ta	Under \<emph\>Consolidate by\</emph\>, select either \<emph\>Row labels\</emph\> or \<emph\>Column labels\</emph\> if the cells of the source data range are not to be consolidated corresponding to the identical position of the cell in the range, but instead according to a matching row label or column label.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3157871				0	ta	To consolidate by row labels or column labels, the label must be contained in the selected source ranges.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3150478				0	ta	The text in the labels must be identical, so that rows or columns can be accurately matched. If the row or column label does not match any that exist in the target range, it will be appended as a new row or column.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3147468				0	ta	The data from the consolidation ranges and target range will be saved when you save the document. If you later open a document in which consolidation has been defined, this data will again be available.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\consolidate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153039				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/12070000.xhp\" name=\"Data - Consolidate\"\>Data - Consolidate\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Assigning Formats by Formula				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145673				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>formats; assigning by formulas\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell formats; assigning by formulas\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>STYLE function example\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell styles;assigning by formulas\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formulas;assigning cell formats\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145673	13			0	ta	\<variable id=\"cellstyle_by_formula\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cellstyle_by_formula.xhp\" name=\"Assigning Formats by Formula\"\>Assigning Formats by Formula\</link\> \</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3150275				0	ta	The STYLE() function can be added to an existing formula in a cell. For example, together with the CURRENT function, you can color a cell depending on its value. The formula =...+STYLE(IF(CURRENT()>3; "Red"; "Green")) applies the cell style "Red" to cells if the value is greater than 3, otherwise the cell style "Green" is applied.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3151385				0	ta	If you would like to apply a formula to all cells in a selected area, you can use the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Find & Replace\</item\> dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3149456				0	ta	Select all the desired cells.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3148797				0	ta	Select the menu command \<emph\>Edit - Find & Replace\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3150767	18			0	ta	For the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Search for\</item\> term, enter: .\<item type=\"literal\"\>*\</item\>            				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153770				0	ta	".*" is a regular expression that designates the contents of the current cell.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153143	20			0	ta	Enter the following formula in the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Replace with\</item\> field: \<item type=\"literal\"\>=&+STYLE(IF(CURRENT()>3;"Red";"Green"))\</item\>            				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3146975				0	ta	The "&" symbol designates the current contents of the \<emph\>Search for\</emph\> field. The line must begin with an equal sign, since it is a formula. It is assumed that the cell styles "Red" and "Green" already exist.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3149262				0	ta	Mark the fields \<link href=\"text/shared/01/02100000.xhp\" name=\"Regular expressions\"\>\<emph\>Regular expressions\</emph\>\</link\> and \<emph\>Current selection only\</emph\>. Click \<emph\>Find All\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3144767				0	ta	All cells with contents that were included in the selection are now highlighted.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3147127				0	ta	Click \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Replace all\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Rotating Tables (Transposing)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154346				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>tables; transposing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>transposing tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inverting tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>swapping tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>columns; swap with rows\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>rows; swapping with columns\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>tables; rotating\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>rotating; tables\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154346				0	ta	\<variable id=\"table_rotate\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/table_rotate.xhp\" name=\"Rotating Tables (Transposing)\"\>Rotating Tables (Transposing)\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3154013				0	ta	In $[officename] Calc, there is a way to "rotate" a spreadsheet so that rows become columns and columns become rows.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153142				0	ta	Select the cell range that you want to transpose.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3153191				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Cut\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3148575				0	ta	Click the cell that is to be the top left cell in the result.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3156286				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Paste Special\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3144764				0	ta	In the dialog, mark \<emph\>Paste all\</emph\> and \<emph\>Transpose\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3155600				0	ta	If you now click OK the columns and rows are transposed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\table_rotate.xhp	0	help	par_id3146969				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/02070000.xhp\" name=\"Paste Special\"\>Paste Special\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Using AutoFormat for Tables				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155132				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>tables; AutoFormat function\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>defining;AutoFormat function for tables\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>AutoFormat function\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formats; automatically formatting spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>automatic formatting in spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>sheets;AutoFormat function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155132				0	ta	\<variable id=\"autoformat\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/autoformat.xhp\" name=\"Using AutoFormat for Tables\"\>Applying Automatic Formatting to a Selected Cell Range\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3149401				0	ta	You can use the AutoFormat feature to quickly apply a format to a sheet or a selected cell range.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_idN10702				0	ta	To Apply an AutoFormat to a Sheet or Selected Cell Range				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_idN106CE				0	ta	Select the cells, including the column and row headers, that you want to format.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D5				0	ta	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - AutoFormat\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3151242				0	ta	To select which properties to include in an AutoFormat, click \<emph\>More\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_idN10715				0	ta	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_idN1075D				0	ta	The format is applied to the selected range of cells.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3149210				0	ta	If you do not see any change in color of the cell contents, choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>View - Value Highlighting\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3155379				0	ta	To Define an AutoFormat for Spreadsheets				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3148868				0	ta	You can define a new AutoFormat that is available to all spreadsheets.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3152985				0	ta	Format a sheet.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3145384				0	ta	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Edit - Select All\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3153815				0	ta	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - AutoFormat\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_idN107A9				0	ta	Click \<emph\>Add\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_idN10760				0	ta	In the \<emph\>Name\</emph\> box of the \<emph\>Add AutoFormat\</emph\> dialog, enter a name for the format.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_idN107C3				0	ta	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\autoformat.xhp	0	help	par_id3159203				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05110000.xhp\" name=\"Format - AutoFormat\"\>Format - AutoFormat\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Referencing Cells by Drag-and-Drop				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154686				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>drag and drop; referencing cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cells; referencing by drag and drop \</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>references;inserting by drag and drop\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>inserting;references, by drag and drop\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154686				0	ta	\<variable id=\"cellreference_dragdrop\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cellreference_dragdrop.xhp\" name=\"Referencing Cells by Drag-and-Drop\"\>Referencing Cells by Drag-and-Drop\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3156444				0	ta	With the help of the Navigator you can reference cells from one sheet to another sheet in the same document or in a different document. The cells can be inserted as a copy, link, or hyperlink. The range to be inserted must be defined with a name in the original file so that it can be inserted in the target file.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3152576				0	ta	Open the document that contains the source cells.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3154011				0	ta	To set the source range as the range, select the cells and choose \<emph\>Insert - Names - Define\</emph\>. Save the source document, and do not close it.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3151073				0	ta	Open the sheet in which you want to insert something.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3154732				0	ta	Open the \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/02110000.xhp\" name=\"Navigator\"\>Navigator\</link\>. In the lower box of the Navigator select the source file.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3150752				0	ta	In the Navigator, the source file object appears under "Range names".				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754				0	ta	Using the \<emph\>Drag Mode\</emph\> icon in Navigator, choose whether you want the reference to be a hyperlink, link, or copy.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3154256				0	ta	Click the name under "Range names" in the Navigator, and drag into the cell of the current sheet where you want to insert the reference.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellreference_dragdrop.xhp	0	help	par_id3149565				0	ta	This method can also be used to insert a range from another sheet of the same document into the current sheet. Select the active document as source in step 4 above.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Shortcut Keys (%PRODUCTNAME Calc Accessibility)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145120				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>accessibility; %PRODUCTNAME Calc shortcuts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>shortcut keys;%PRODUCTNAME Calc accessibility\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145120				0	ta	\<variable id=\"keyboard\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/keyboard.xhp\" name=\"Shortcut Keys (%PRODUCTNAME Calc Accessibility)\"\>Shortcut Keys (\<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc Accessibility)\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3154760				0	ta	Refer also to the lists of shortcut keys for \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc and \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> in general.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153360				0	ta	Cell Selection Mode				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150870				0	ta	\<image id=\"img_id3150439\" src=\"formula/res/refinp1.png\" width=\"0.1327inch\" height=\"0.1327inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3150439\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319				0	ta	In a text box that has a button to minimize the dialog, press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>F2\</item\> to enter the cell selection mode. Select any number of cells, then press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>F2\</item\> again to show the dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3145272				0	ta	In the cell selection mode, you can use the common navigation keys to select cells.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148646				0	ta	Controlling the Outline				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3146120				0	ta	You can use the keyboard in \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/12080000.xhp\" name=\"Outline\"\>Outline\</link\>:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3147394				0	ta	Press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>F6\</item\> or \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Shift+F6\</item\> until the vertical or horizontal outline window has the focus.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3149379				0	ta	\<item type=\"keycode\"\>Tab\</item\> - cycle through all visible buttons from top to bottom or from left to right.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3156286				0	ta	\<item type=\"keycode\"\>Shift+Tab\</item\> - cycle through all visible buttons in the opposite direction.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3149403				0	ta	\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command+1 to Command+8\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl+1 to Ctrl+8\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - show all levels up to the specified number; hide all higher levels.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150329				0	ta	Use \<item type=\"keycode\"\>+\</item\> or \<item type=\"keycode\"\>-\</item\> to show or hide the focused outline group.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3155446				0	ta	Press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> to activate the focused button.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253				0	ta	Use \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Up\</item\>, \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Down\</item\>, \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Left\</item\>, or \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Right\</item\> arrow to cycle through all buttons in the current level.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147343				0	ta	Selecting a Drawing Object or a Graphic				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_idN107AA				0	ta	Choose View - Toolbars - Drawing to open the Drawing toolbar.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3155333				0	ta	Press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>F6\</item\> until the \<emph\>Drawing\</emph\> toolbar is selected.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150345				0	ta	If the selection tool is active, press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Enter. This selects the first drawing object or graphic in the sheet.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3159240				0	ta	With \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+F6 you set the focus to the document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3155379				0	ta	Now you can use \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Tab\</item\> to select the next drawing object or graphic and \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Shift+Tab\</item\> to select the previous one.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ta	Printing Sheets in Landscape Format				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153418				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>printing; sheet selections\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>sheets; printing in landscape\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>printing; landscape\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>landscape printing\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153418				0	ta	\<variable id=\"print_landscape\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/print_landscape.xhp\" name=\"Printing Sheets in Landscape Format\"\>Printing Sheets in Landscape Format\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id3149257				0	ta	In order to print a sheet you have a number of interactive options available under \<emph\>View - Page Break Preview\</emph\>. Drag the delimiter lines to define the range of printed cells on each page.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id3153963				0	ta	To print in landscape format, proceed as follows:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id3154020				0	ta	Go to the sheet to be printed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id3150786				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>Format - Page\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id3150089	5			0	ta	The command is not visible if the sheet has been opened with write protection on. In that case, click the \<emph\>Edit File \</emph\>icon on the \<emph\>Standard\</emph\> bar.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id3166430				0	ta	Select the \<emph\>Page\</emph\> tab. Select the \<emph\>Landscape\</emph\> paper format and click OK.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id3150885				0	ta	Choose \<emph\>File - Print\</emph\>. You will see the \<emph\>Print\</emph\> dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id3156288				0	ta	Depending on the printer driver and the operating system, it may be necessary to click the \<emph\>Properties\</emph\> button and to change your printer to landscape format there.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id3149404	9			0	ta	In the \<emph\>Print \</emph\>dialog in the \<emph\>General\</emph\> tab page, select the contents to be printed:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id3153305	10			0	ta	               \<emph\>All sheets\</emph\> - All sheets will be printed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id3148871	12			0	ta	               \<emph\>Selected sheets\</emph\> - Only the selected sheets will be printed. All sheets whose names (at the bottom on the sheet tabs) are selected will be printed. By pressing \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> while clicking a sheet name you can change this selection.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id3764763				0	ta	               \<emph\>Selected cells\</emph\> - All selected cells are printed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id5538804				0	ta	From all the paper pages that result from the above selection, you can select the range of paper pages to be printed:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id14343				0	ta	               \<emph\>All pages\</emph\> - Print all resulting pages.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id3148699	11			0	ta	               \<emph\>Pages\</emph\> - Enter the pages to be printed. The pages will also be numbered from the first sheet onwards. If you see in the Page Break Preview that Sheet1 will be printed on 4 pages and you want to print the first two pages of Sheet2, enter 5-6 here.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id3145076				0	ta	If under \<emph\>Format - Print ranges\</emph\> you have defined one or more print ranges, only the contents of these print ranges will be printed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id3156019				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/03100000.xhp\" name=\"View - Page Break Preview\"\>View - Page Break Preview\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_landscape.xhp	0	help	par_id8254646				0	ta	\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/printranges.xhp\"\>Defining Print Ranges on a Sheet\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
