helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166137				0	ta	\<emph\>DataY\</emph\> represents the Y Data array.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166156				0	ta	\<emph\>DataX\</emph\> (optional) represents the X Data array.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166176				0	ta	\<emph\>NewDataX\</emph\> (optional) represents the array of the X data, which are used for recalculating values.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166196				0	ta	\<emph\>LinearType\</emph\>(Optional). If LinearType = 0, then lines will be calculated through the zero point. Otherwise, offset lines will also be calculated. The default is LinearType <> 0.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN11D2F				0	ta	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN12019				0	ta	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#moreontop\"/\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166231				0	ta	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166245				0	ta	Select a spreadsheet range in which the trend data will appear. Select the function. Enter the output data or select it with the mouse. Mark the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> field. click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. The trend data calculated from the output data is displayed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3166317				0	ta	\<bookmark_value\>GROWTH function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>exponential trends in arrays\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166317				0	ta	GROWTH				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166346				0	ta	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_VARIATION\"\>Calculates the points of an exponential trend in an array.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3166364				0	ta	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166377				0	ta	GROWTH(DataY; DataX; NewDataX; FunctionType)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166392				0	ta	\<emph\>DataY\</emph\> represents the Y Data array.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166411				0	ta	\<emph\>DataX\</emph\> (optional) represents the X Data array.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3173797				0	ta	\<emph\>NewDataX\</emph\> (optional) represents the X data array, in which the values are recalculated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3173817				0	ta	\<emph\>FunctionType\</emph\>(optional). If FunctionType = 0, functions in the form y = m^x will be calculated. Otherwise, y = b*m^x functions will be calculated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN11DFD				0	ta	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN12113				0	ta	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#moreontop\"/\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3173839				0	ta	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3173852				0	ta	This function returns an array and is handled in the same way as the other array functions. Select a range where you want the answers to appear and select the function. Select DataY. Enter any other parameters, mark \<emph\>Array\</emph\> and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
